.TH JABRAW 1 "September 2026" "jabraw" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
jabraw \- media keys and tray menu for Jabra headsets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jabraw
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The multi-function button on Jabra headsets does nothing on Linux. The
Link 380 dongle declares the relevant bits as Constant in its HID report
descriptor, and
.BR hid-input (4)
skips constant fields, so the kernel allocates no key code and evdev never
sees the press.
.PP
.B jabraw
reads the raw hidraw reports instead and forwards the button to the active
MPRIS player. It runs as a tray icon using the StatusNotifierItem protocol,
showing battery level and charging state, the current player and track,
volume and mute for speaker and microphone, and output device selection.
.PP
Paired over Bluetooth rather than through the dongle, the same data is read
over an RFCOMM connection that BlueZ establishes. Devices are matched by
vendor id, USB 0b0e and Bluetooth 0067, not by model.
.PP
The volume rocker is deliberately left alone. The kernel maps it to
.B KEY_VOLUMEUP
and
.BR KEY_VOLUMEDOWN ,
and the desktop already acts on those; handling it here as well would change
the volume twice per press.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version, \-V
Print the version and exit. Autostart runs the installed binary, so this is
how to tell which build is actually running.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Print every raw HID report and the bits that changed, with their usage names.
This is how further buttons are identified.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tray
Run without a tray icon, handling keys only. Useful where no
StatusNotifierItem host is present.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Exit without a word when another instance already holds the D-Bus name,
instead of reporting its state. Used by the autostart entry and the systemd
unit, which can both fire for the same session.
.TP
.BI \-\-write\-icons " directory"
Write the application icons as PNG files and exit. Used when building the
package.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-jabraw.rules
Grants the logged-in user access to the Jabra hidraw node through a uaccess
ACL. The file has to sort before
.IR 73\-seat\-late.rules ,
which is what turns the tag into an ACL. The same file tags the device for
systemd and pulls
.B jabraw.service
in through
.BR SYSTEMD_USER_WANTS ,
so plugging a Jabra device in starts the daemon in a session where it is not
running yet. A headset paired over Bluetooth is caught the same way, through
the input device bluetoothd creates for its AVRCP keys.
.TP
.I /usr/share/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/50\-jabraw\-no\-suspend.conf
Keeps WirePlumber from suspending the Jabra audio nodes. Without it the
dongle tears down the USB audio stream while idle, and the next play takes
long enough to look like a lost key press.
.TP
.I /etc/xdg/autostart/jabraw.desktop
Starts jabraw on login, for the case where a device is already there and no
udev event will announce it.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/jabraw.service
Started by the udev rule when a Jabra device appears. It is deliberately not
enabled, since the device pulls it in and login is covered by XDG autostart;
a second daemon would end immediately over the D\-Bus name anyway. A
.B Requisite=
on
.B graphical\-session.target
keeps it from starting outside a graphical session, so a plain SSH login
raises no daemon without a tray.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exits 0 when another instance already holds the D-Bus name
.IR io.github.michidold.Jabraw ,
after reporting the current state \(em or without a word under
.B \-\-quiet
\(em so autostart does not treat it as a crash.
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at https://github.com/michidold/jabraw/issues
.SH AUTHOR
Michael Dold
